00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:16.310
In this talk, I want to show
you the practical aspects

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:17.810
of multi-tenancy
that, you know, you

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:19.550
can go home and start using.

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:22.040
But first, we must
define the terminology

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:23.840
that we're going to use.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.170
And I want to start with
what is multi-tenancy?

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:29.840
And in the software
context, the multi-tenancy

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.060
means a single of software
running somewhere,

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.940
and it serves multiple
tenants, right?

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:37.400
You can simply say, any
websites that you folks

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:40.650
are using probably is a
multi-tenant software, right?

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:42.950
So today's topic is
Kubernetes, and that's

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:45.220
why we're going to
talk about Kubernetes

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:48.840
as a multi-tenant
system and analyze it.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.380
In Kubernetes' terms,
Kubernetes' multi-tenancy

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.720
is providing isolation
and fair resource

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:57.860
sharing between multiple
users in a single cluster.

00:03:57.860 --> 00:03:58.950
This part is important.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:01.680
It's important that it
is in a single cluster.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:03.740
So at the end,
we're going to talk

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:08.600
about how the multiple
tenants in a single cluster

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:12.470
live happily without stepping
on each other's toes.

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:14.660
And before we talk
about multi-tenancy,

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:18.269
I first want to talk about
trust and why is it important.

00:04:18.269 --> 00:04:20.630
So let's go through
these things.

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:23.990
Anyone here completely
trust all these things

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:26.430
in their deployment pipelines?

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:27.980
Like, they totally trust it?

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:28.970
I don't think so.

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:33.020
So a lot of things that you
use to deploy your software,

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:36.890
run your software, or
build your software mostly

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:40.400
comes from people that you
don't exactly know, right?

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:42.290
There are papers,
scientific papers,

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:43.740
that I cited on
the bottom right.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:46.460
You can take a look at it
about trusting compilers.

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:49.510
And, you know, verifiable
operating systems

00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:50.960
is a space on its own.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:52.880
And then mostly
you would probably

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:55.430
use open source dependencies,
and your deployment pipeline

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:57.440
is probably Jenkins
or something else.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:04:59.000
And then there are
container runtimes

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:00.041
that you folks are using.

00:05:00.041 --> 00:05:02.810
Docker is, for instance,
or Containerd, or whatever.

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:05.580
So do you actually
trust these runtimes?

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:09.250
So it's not a binary
or anything like that.

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:10.370
It's not a binary choice.

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:13.990
It's mostly like you're probably
somewhere on the spectrum.

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:17.990
So we classify these levels
of software multi-tenancy

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:21.890
in terms of trusted,
semi-trusted, and non-trusted.

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:24.230
So in the trusted
case, everything

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:26.610
comes from an audited
source repository, right?

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:28.940
It's basically pretty
much what Google does,

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:30.870
and the compiler is trusted.

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:33.860
The build system is trusted,
and even some of the hardware

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:34.680
is trusted.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:36.850
But most people are
not there, right?

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:40.790
Most of us are probably in some
place that is semi-trusted.

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:43.290
We have some trust
components that we rely on,

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:46.130
and we build our security
models around it.

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:48.380
So now, let's talk about
some of the Kubernetes Engine

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:51.980
multi-tenancy primitives.

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:54.200
If you're already doing
multi-tenancy today,

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:57.190
you're probably
segmenting your tenants

00:05:57.190 --> 00:06:00.040
in your clusters probably
based on either creating

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:01.630
a separate cluster
for each tenant

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:04.270
or creating a namespace
for each tenant.

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:07.690
And both options
can be OK, and let's

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:09.670
dive into how are
they different.

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:11.440
In the cluster per
tenant case, you're

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:13.930
basically creating
separate control planes.

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:16.270
By control plane, I
mean the Kubernetes API.

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:18.400
Anytime you run kubectl
get or something

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:21.250
like that, you hit the
Kubernetes control plane.

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:24.280
In this case, just because
there are separate clusters,

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:26.170
you've got a separate
control plane.

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:28.720
And you might actually be
getting separate networks

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:31.270
as well, if every cluster
lives its own VPC,

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:34.380
virtual private network, right?

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:35.950
This can be still
useful if you're

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:40.300
separating your clusters between
dev stage and production.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:41.800
However, this has
some shortcomings,

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:43.720
such as if you have
a lot of users,

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:48.190
then you need tools
that let you manage

00:06:48.190 --> 00:06:50.680
tens and hundreds of clusters.

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:53.110
And potentially, this is
not a fun job to be at.

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:57.010
And you need to worry
about the fragmentation

00:06:57.010 --> 00:06:59.170
issues, the diverging
configuration that

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:00.760
happens in the cluster.

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:03.670
And every time someone
asks for a runtime,

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:07.290
you need to go actually
provision that cluster.

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:09.580
However, namespace is a
little bit better than that,

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:12.860
and it's mostly because
Kubernetes is basically

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:14.360
providing a lot
of policy objects

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:17.030
that are native to
Kubernetes namespaces.

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:18.680
So namespace is
the building block

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:22.550
that lets us build our
multi-tenant infrastructure

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:24.210
on Kubernetes.

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:26.720
And again, the pros of
this is that anytime

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:29.660
you run a cluster with multiple
tenants, any configuration

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:32.420
or setting or, you know,
off-time reliability

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:35.420
concerns that you apply to that
cluster is shared by everyone.

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:37.580
Anytime you add an
add-on, extension

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:39.380
is, again, shared by everyone.

00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:42.230
And in this case, since you're
sharing the control plane,

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:45.834
they'll benefit from the
same security measures.

00:07:45.834 --> 00:07:48.250
So you know, we're going to
talk about a lot of Kubernetes

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:49.510
security primitives today.

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:51.720
I just listed some of them here.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:54.100
I mainly categorize them
under access control,

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:56.830
resource isolation,
and runtime isolation.

00:07:56.830 --> 00:08:00.050
And actually, we're going
to recategorize them later.

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:01.900
So now let's evaluate
some of the common use

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:03.670
cases with Kubernetes
multi-tenancy,

00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:05.290
like what are the
things that you

00:08:05.290 --> 00:08:07.300
might be doing so
that you would require

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:09.494
a multi-tenant
environment on Kubernetes?

00:08:09.494 --> 00:08:11.410
We're going to look at
three different models.

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:14.170
One of them is Enterprise,
where you're in a company

00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:17.470
and every team is
potentially a tenant.

00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:19.630
And then there is the
Software as a Service, which

00:08:19.630 --> 00:08:21.580
most users don't actually
see in Kubernetes,

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:23.894
but the infrastructure
still runs on Kubernetes.

00:08:23.894 --> 00:08:26.560
And then there's Kubernetes as a
Service, where you're basically

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:30.610
accepting workloads from
people that you don't know.

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:33.640
So let's focus on the
Enterprise model first.

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:35.530
In the Enterprise
model, all users

00:08:35.530 --> 00:08:36.830
are from the same company.

00:08:36.830 --> 00:08:39.010
So if anybody is
doing something wrong,

00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:41.330
you can go fire them, right?

00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:45.530
In this case, everybody
gets its own namespace.

00:08:45.530 --> 00:08:48.160
In the diagram you
can see up here,

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:50.860
there is a cluster
admin that potentially

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:53.140
has the full permissions
on the cluster.

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:55.360
And then there
are various users.

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:59.080
Each emoji here is a different
team, and then in a team,

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:01.930
there is a Namespace Admin
that has a little bit more

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:03.920
permissions on that namespace.

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:06.100
So to achieve these
personas, we're

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:08.290
looking at creating
cluster roles.

00:09:08.290 --> 00:09:10.660
So again, the Cluster
Admin has full admin.

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:14.020
Namespace Admin manages the
users in that namespace.

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:16.895
And then the user
basically can apply,

00:09:16.895 --> 00:09:19.810
let's say, pods and workloads
and load balancers and stuff

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:20.450
like that.

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:23.150
But they cannot actually
edit the policies.

00:09:23.150 --> 00:09:26.900
So we're going to come
back to the policies later.

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:30.410
In this case, all
the source code

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:32.240
is coming from a
single code repository,

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:35.930
such as your company's source
depots, maybe a vanilla

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:38.870
container isolation you get
with Containerd or Docker

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:41.250
or something like
that might be OK.

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:43.400
But if it's not, if
you're running components

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:45.860
that you don't trust
much, you might

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:47.750
be considering to
run on something

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:49.640
like gVisor for Sandboxing.

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:51.950
Or you can use Linux
security primitives,

00:09:51.950 --> 00:09:57.470
such as seccomp, AppArmor,
SELinux, and stuff like that.

00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:00.394
So as far as the network
isolation is concerned,

00:10:00.394 --> 00:10:02.810
we're going to come back to
how network isolation is done,

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:05.320
but I basically want to say
that each namespace probably

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:07.540
should be able to talk to
anything in that namespace.

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:09.670
And outside that
namespace, you should

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:13.931
be worried about who's calling
your applications, right?

00:10:13.931 --> 00:10:15.430
So the next thing
I'm going to touch

00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:17.260
is the Software as
a Service model.

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:20.129
This is a little bit different.

00:10:20.129 --> 00:10:21.670
Basically, in this
case, the consumer

00:10:21.670 --> 00:10:24.450
deploys their app through
a custom control plane.

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:27.760
So the users of your
Software as a Service

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:29.760
does not know Kubernetes much.

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:32.800
Maybe they are not actually
seeing Kubernetes at all.

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:35.950
In this case, after
they deploy your app,

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:37.450
after they deploy
their app, they're

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:39.310
basically connecting
to the app directly,

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:41.710
so they're not seeing
Kubernetes in the middle at all.

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:45.130
So Kubernetes is, in this case,
is an implementation detail.

00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:47.680
So this is not very interesting
to Kubernetes, actually.

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:49.120
An example of this
can be, you can

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:51.682
imagine WordPress.com
anytime you've probably

00:10:51.682 --> 00:10:53.890
assumed that, theoretically,
you go to WordPress.com.

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:55.973
You create a blog, and
then WordPress.com probably

00:10:55.973 --> 00:10:57.700
creates some pods for you.

00:10:57.700 --> 00:11:00.370
And then you directly talk
to your WordPress admin

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:00.876
page, right?

00:11:00.876 --> 00:11:02.500
You don't see Kubernetes
in the middle.

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:04.960
But someone, in this
case, the Cluster Admin,

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:06.940
has access to the
Kubernetes API.

00:11:06.940 --> 00:11:09.730
And basically,
there is a SaaS API

00:11:09.730 --> 00:11:13.740
that is a trusted
client of Kubernetes.

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:16.490
So in this case,
the tenant workload

00:11:16.490 --> 00:11:18.920
can actually have
untrusted pieces.

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:21.110
And let's say you
have a WordPress blog,

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:22.940
and you are
installing extensions.

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:26.351
And extensions are potentially
arbitrary code execution,

00:11:26.351 --> 00:11:26.850
right?

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:29.450
So in this case, you
may require something

00:11:29.450 --> 00:11:32.690
like Sandboxing again, with
gVisor or some other runtime.

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:37.520
And the last model that
we're going to look at

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:39.950
is the Kubernetes
as a Service model.

00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:42.550
You can call this containers
as a service as well.

00:11:42.550 --> 00:11:44.300
You know, you're running
a hosting company

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:46.700
that you're accepting
containers from everybody.

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:48.530
What can go wrong?

00:11:48.530 --> 00:11:50.750
In this case, tenants,
again, you know,

00:11:50.750 --> 00:11:51.950
have different link spaces.

00:11:51.950 --> 00:11:54.950
But in this case, they're
not seeing each other at all.

00:11:54.950 --> 00:11:58.460
So you have a little bit more
stronger isolation requirements

00:11:58.460 --> 00:12:00.860
than the Enterprise or the
SaaS that we looked at.

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:03.890
And the isolation of
[INAUDIBLE] can easily

00:12:03.890 --> 00:12:07.250
be achieved with something
like Kubernetes RBAC.

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:09.950
Again, the network
isolation requires stuff

00:12:09.950 --> 00:12:13.130
like the Sandbox pods and
the multi-tenant networking

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:15.470
so that people are
actually not stepping

00:12:15.470 --> 00:12:17.600
on each other's networking
and potentially snooping

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:20.940
on each other's networking.

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:24.300
So let's talk a little bit about
what are these policy objects

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:26.790
and features that
Kubernetes provides to us

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:31.470
so that we can use them in our
companies and in our clusters.

00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:34.380
So we've previously talked about
these features, like admission

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:38.010
control, RBAC, and quotas,
and pod security policies,

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:39.330
and a bunch of other things.

00:12:39.330 --> 00:12:43.230
So I'm actually going to
reorganize this a little bit

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:44.220
like this.

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:46.950
The first category
is auth related,

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:48.840
and the second
category is going to be

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:51.009
the scheduling-related
primitives.

00:12:51.009 --> 00:12:52.300
But it actually doesn't matter.

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:55.740
It's just so that we can
easily go through them.

00:12:55.740 --> 00:13:00.410
So let me go through the
auth-related features first.

00:13:00.410 --> 00:13:02.510
If you're familiar with
Kubernetes authentication,

00:13:02.510 --> 00:13:04.670
authorization, and
admission, this

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:07.920
is basically how an object
comes to Kubernetes API,

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:11.000
and from the Kubernetes
API, makes it through etcd,

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:12.170
which is where it's stored.

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:14.586
You don't see the etcd in
Google Kubernetes Engine's case.

00:13:14.586 --> 00:13:18.110
But basically, when you're
on your laptop or let's

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:20.690
say where there's a pod or a
program calling the Kubernetes

00:13:20.690 --> 00:13:23.540
API, first we
authorize that request.

00:13:23.540 --> 00:13:25.160
And the way we
authorize that request

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:29.480
is either through Kubernetes
RBAC or Google Cloud IAM.

00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:31.970
In this case, if you're a
Google user, with your Gmail

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:34.580
or whatever, you can be
authorized through GKE

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:37.370
using the IAM authorizer.

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:40.310
And if you're not, if you're
like a Kubernetes service

00:13:40.310 --> 00:13:43.250
account, which is like
some identity that

00:13:43.250 --> 00:13:46.280
are given to pods, then you're
probably going through RBAC.

00:13:46.280 --> 00:13:48.830
So if RBAC allows you,
then the next thing

00:13:48.830 --> 00:13:52.260
is the admission control,
which we'll talk about later.

00:13:52.260 --> 00:13:56.480
So let's dig a little bit
deep into the authorizer.

00:13:56.480 --> 00:14:00.290
So Kubernetes RBAC is letting
you figure out which users

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:02.930
or which groups or which
service accounts have access

00:14:02.930 --> 00:14:07.010
to which operations and
which API resources on which

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:09.860
namespaces.

00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:13.840
So look a little bit deeper
into Kubernetes RBAC.

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:15.890
So two use cases
for Kubernetes RBAC

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:18.080
is you're either
giving people access

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:21.440
or you're giving Kubernetes
service accounts access.

00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:24.290
And Kubernetes service
accounts are used by the pods.

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:27.740
So you might have some
controllers trying

00:14:27.740 --> 00:14:29.450
to access your Kubernetes API.

00:14:29.450 --> 00:14:32.990
And in this case, the API will
authorize a Kubernetes service

00:14:32.990 --> 00:14:33.950
account.

00:14:33.950 --> 00:14:36.050
So four key concepts
in Kubernetes RBAC

00:14:36.050 --> 00:14:38.810
are cluster role,
which is you're just

00:14:38.810 --> 00:14:41.420
defining a persona,
a set of permissions

00:14:41.420 --> 00:14:44.270
that people can
use cluster-wide.

00:14:44.270 --> 00:14:46.910
And there's a
namespace-scoped version

00:14:46.910 --> 00:14:49.410
of that called just role.

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:51.360
And there is a
cluster role binding,

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:54.440
which lets you bind
cluster roles to users.

00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:57.380
In this case, a user could
be just Google users,

00:14:57.380 --> 00:15:00.140
Google groups, Cloud
IAM service accounts,

00:15:00.140 --> 00:15:02.780
or again, Kubernetes
service accounts.

00:15:02.780 --> 00:15:06.770
And the role binding is the
namespace version of that.

00:15:06.770 --> 00:15:08.640
So here is an example, RBAC.

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:11.560
This must be the first
YAML we're seeing today.

00:15:11.560 --> 00:15:14.170
This is the example
cluster role,

00:15:14.170 --> 00:15:15.820
an example cluster role binding.

00:15:15.820 --> 00:15:19.600
And if I look here, I'm defining
a namespace creator persona,

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:23.230
which is a namespace
creator cluster role.

00:15:23.230 --> 00:15:26.140
And then this namespace creator
can do a get, list, watch,

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:28.510
create, delete, whatever
all these objects,

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:30.660
only on the namespace, right?

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:33.820
And when I go to the right side,
I see the cluster role binding.

00:15:33.820 --> 00:15:37.621
And this cluster role binding is
assigned to a particular user.

00:15:37.621 --> 00:15:38.620
That's my email address.

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:41.110
Don't send me spam, please.

00:15:41.110 --> 00:15:43.690
So just like I'm adding
myself as a Google user,

00:15:43.690 --> 00:15:46.390
I can add a group, I can add
a Kubernetes service account,

00:15:46.390 --> 00:15:47.142
and so forth.

00:15:47.142 --> 00:15:48.850
So this is how you
give access to someone

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:51.100
to do something on your
Kubernetes cluster using

00:15:51.100 --> 00:15:52.750
Kubernetes RBAC.

00:15:52.750 --> 00:15:54.550
So I'm going to jump
off RBAC, and I'm

00:15:54.550 --> 00:15:57.370
going to talk a little
bit about the GKE IAM,

00:15:57.370 --> 00:15:59.400
basically, how we do Cloud IAM.

00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:01.900
This is a little bit different,
and it's an alternative

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:05.020
to Kubernetes RBAC.

00:16:05.020 --> 00:16:08.020
So this is mostly practical for
giving Google users and Google

00:16:08.020 --> 00:16:11.140
groups access to all
clusters in your project.

00:16:11.140 --> 00:16:12.730
Let's say you
temporarily need to add

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:16.570
your colleagues to all clusters
as a viewer permission.

00:16:16.570 --> 00:16:17.710
You can easily use that.

00:16:17.710 --> 00:16:21.140
So we provide four IAM
roles today that we created.

00:16:21.140 --> 00:16:23.540
Admin is like basically,
you can do everything.

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:25.430
Viewer is you can
view everything.

00:16:25.430 --> 00:16:28.510
Cluster Admin is you can only
manage the cluster's lifecycle

00:16:28.510 --> 00:16:31.060
and create, delete clusters
and upgrade them and so forth.

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:34.420
The developer is you just
look inside the cluster

00:16:34.420 --> 00:16:37.840
and edit objects and create new
objects within that cluster,

00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:39.970
and you don't know
about GKE at all,

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:41.710
so that's just
Kubernetes related.

00:16:41.710 --> 00:16:44.440
And you can actually
use IAM custom roles

00:16:44.440 --> 00:16:46.570
to curate your own
more interesting roles.

00:16:46.570 --> 00:16:48.670
Again, like the
Namespace Admin I just

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:50.650
created using
Kubernetes RBAC, it

00:16:50.650 --> 00:16:53.500
can also be created
using Cloud IAM.

00:16:53.500 --> 00:16:55.630
So if you're interested
in project-wide access,

00:16:55.630 --> 00:16:58.440
this is what you're looking at.

00:16:58.440 --> 00:16:59.947
So an example of GKE IAM--

00:16:59.947 --> 00:17:01.530
I'm sure if you're
actually using GKE,

00:17:01.530 --> 00:17:03.170
you've probably
seen this before.

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:05.520
You know, you add an IAM
policy binding on the project.

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:07.950
You give some user
or a group access,

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:09.619
and you can specify
which role you want.

00:17:09.619 --> 00:17:13.109
This is a gcloud CLI.

00:17:13.109 --> 00:17:16.160
And next, I'm going to talk
about the admission controls.

00:17:16.160 --> 00:17:19.670
Has anyone here actually written
an admission controller before?

00:17:19.670 --> 00:17:21.359
I'm curious.

00:17:21.359 --> 00:17:21.900
No, wow.

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:22.630
That's amazing.

00:17:22.630 --> 00:17:25.619
So admission controllers are
a primitive in Kubernetes

00:17:25.619 --> 00:17:27.810
that the requests go through.

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:30.540
So anytime you create
a pod, Kubernetes

00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:32.760
calls a bunch of
admission plugins.

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:37.110
And each plugin tells Kubernetes
this pod is OK or not.

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:40.500
So any time it happens,
basically anytime you

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:42.060
submit a request
that is controlled

00:17:42.060 --> 00:17:43.830
by a particular
admission plugin,

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:46.380
your request goes through a
bunch of admission plugins

00:17:46.380 --> 00:17:47.490
like this.

00:17:47.490 --> 00:17:50.160
So after the admission
plugins succeed--

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:53.040
they basically run in
serial one after another--

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:58.310
they go into the etcd and your
pod is created after that.

00:17:58.310 --> 00:18:00.950
So admission
controls are actually

00:18:00.950 --> 00:18:03.270
compiled into the
Kubernetes API server.

00:18:03.270 --> 00:18:06.320
So this means you actually
cannot go and change

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:08.360
the admission plugins
that already exist.

00:18:08.360 --> 00:18:10.400
And in Google
Kubernetes Engine, we

00:18:10.400 --> 00:18:15.020
don't let you change the
admission plugins that

00:18:15.020 --> 00:18:16.245
are already enabled.

00:18:16.245 --> 00:18:17.620
So in this list,
you're basically

00:18:17.620 --> 00:18:19.460
seeing 15 admission
plugins that probably

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:21.020
correspond to
Kubernetes features

00:18:21.020 --> 00:18:23.040
that you might be already using.

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:25.010
So these are already
enabled on GKE today.

00:18:25.010 --> 00:18:28.970
You know, the list may change
tomorrow, or in a new version.

00:18:28.970 --> 00:18:31.820
But today, in a snapshot, it
basically looks like this.

00:18:34.365 --> 00:18:36.240
So I said that admission
plugins are actually

00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:39.540
compiled into the Kubernetes
binary, but you can--

00:18:39.540 --> 00:18:42.600
I think as of Kubernetes
1.9 or something

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:45.900
like that-- you can write
your own Kubernetes admission

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:46.770
plugins.

00:18:46.770 --> 00:18:50.490
And the way this works is you
create a pod, and then pod

00:18:50.490 --> 00:18:52.704
goes through a bunch
of admission plugins.

00:18:52.704 --> 00:18:54.120
And then there is
something called

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:56.400
mutating admission webhook.

00:18:56.400 --> 00:19:00.060
And then that calls your webhook
that you might have registered,

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:03.840
and in that case, you actually
change the API objects

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:05.320
before they are created.

00:19:05.320 --> 00:19:07.060
So let's say I'm
just trolling people.

00:19:07.060 --> 00:19:09.332
And every time
they create a pod,

00:19:09.332 --> 00:19:11.790
I'm going to change their pod
name, or something like that.

00:19:11.790 --> 00:19:14.490
You can do something like this
using the mutating admission

00:19:14.490 --> 00:19:15.679
webhooks.

00:19:15.679 --> 00:19:17.220
And the validating
admission webhooks

00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:19.720
are basically just if
the pod is good or not.

00:19:19.720 --> 00:19:22.320
So you can write your
own admission plugin.

00:19:22.320 --> 00:19:25.080
If the pod doesn't look good
to you, you can just reject it.

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:27.890
And the users will see
the rejection error

00:19:27.890 --> 00:19:29.310
in their kubectl or whatever.

00:19:29.310 --> 00:19:32.790
So this is a way to extend
Kubernetes authorization model.

00:19:32.790 --> 00:19:37.030
If you don't want someone to
do something on your cluster,

00:19:37.030 --> 00:19:38.970
and if the authorization
is not enough,

00:19:38.970 --> 00:19:40.650
you can use the
admission controls

00:19:40.650 --> 00:19:43.440
to enforce more strict
policies at your companies

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:46.750
by writing simple webhooks.

00:19:46.750 --> 00:19:49.150
So let's talk a little bit
about the PodSecurityPolicy.

00:19:49.150 --> 00:19:51.350
This is a relatively
new feature again.

00:19:51.350 --> 00:19:56.020
This restricts host access, like
host file system, host network,

00:19:56.020 --> 00:19:59.140
the ports, the
process ID namespace,

00:19:59.140 --> 00:20:01.810
and stuff like that to the pods.

00:20:01.810 --> 00:20:04.840
So let's say you want to
create a privileged container.

00:20:04.840 --> 00:20:06.580
This actually prevents
you from stating

00:20:06.580 --> 00:20:10.120
a policy that prevents
other people from creating

00:20:10.120 --> 00:20:11.270
privileged containers.

00:20:11.270 --> 00:20:13.120
So if you hate
privileged containers,

00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:15.160
if you hate containers
running as root,

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:17.320
this is exactly what
you're looking for.

00:20:17.320 --> 00:20:20.330
In this diagram, I'm looking at
the pod spec coming through--

00:20:20.330 --> 00:20:22.550
coming to the admission
control in the API,

00:20:22.550 --> 00:20:23.920
and then the admission control--

00:20:23.920 --> 00:20:26.380
PodSecurityPolicy
admission controller

00:20:26.380 --> 00:20:28.630
checks the pod security
specs that you have.

00:20:28.630 --> 00:20:30.470
And then based on that,
it makes a decision.

00:20:33.180 --> 00:20:35.460
Again, in this
example, on the left,

00:20:35.460 --> 00:20:37.740
I have a PodSecurityPolicy
that this

00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:39.990
allows the privileged
pods, and this allows

00:20:39.990 --> 00:20:42.630
the pods running as root user.

00:20:42.630 --> 00:20:44.310
And on the right,
on the bottom right,

00:20:44.310 --> 00:20:47.250
I'm trying to deploy a pod
with security context that

00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:49.110
has privileged equals true.

00:20:49.110 --> 00:20:52.390
And this pod will be rejected.

00:20:52.390 --> 00:20:54.780
So the next thing
is network policy.

00:20:54.780 --> 00:20:56.490
This is, again, a
relatively new feature,

00:20:56.490 --> 00:20:59.850
and I think about
six months ago,

00:20:59.850 --> 00:21:03.940
this has gone GA on
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:21:03.940 --> 00:21:05.940
So this, again,
defines which pods

00:21:05.940 --> 00:21:08.070
can talk to which other pods.

00:21:08.070 --> 00:21:13.040
And this can be determined based
on which namespaces the pods

00:21:13.040 --> 00:21:15.390
live on, or this can be
depending on what labels

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:18.780
the pods have, or you can
specify certain IP ranges that

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:21.390
the pods cannot talk
to or get traffic from.

00:21:21.390 --> 00:21:24.030
So network policy is
basically a firewall

00:21:24.030 --> 00:21:26.640
written in Kubernetes
language for your pods.

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:28.860
So we're offering this
through Calico Network Plugin.

00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:30.901
If you will create a
cluster today with dash dash

00:21:30.901 --> 00:21:34.460
enable network policy,
you'll be getting that.

00:21:34.460 --> 00:21:38.680
An example network
policy I have here is

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:42.020
I'm selecting a bunch of
pods that have the label app

00:21:42.020 --> 00:21:43.780
equals mysql--

00:21:43.780 --> 00:21:44.950
you can see in the blue.

00:21:44.950 --> 00:21:47.680
And then the next
thing I do is I allow--

00:21:47.680 --> 00:21:49.840
the ingress keyword
here means "allow"--

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:53.890
traffic from the pods that have
the labels app equals frontend.

00:21:53.890 --> 00:21:58.960
So in this case, my frontend
pods talk to my SQL pods.

00:21:58.960 --> 00:22:01.279
So what I did was I
created a repository

00:22:01.279 --> 00:22:03.070
for Kubernetes network
policies a while ago

00:22:03.070 --> 00:22:04.070
at the previous KubeCon.

00:22:04.070 --> 00:22:06.370
You can find the link here.

00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:08.025
What I have in this
repository is just

00:22:08.025 --> 00:22:09.970
a bunch of examples
of all the cool things

00:22:09.970 --> 00:22:12.152
you can do with network
policy, and I really

00:22:12.152 --> 00:22:14.110
encourage you to use
Kubernetes network policy,

00:22:14.110 --> 00:22:16.270
because it is right
now not utilized well.

00:22:16.270 --> 00:22:18.610
And it is pretty
good if you're just

00:22:18.610 --> 00:22:23.560
looking for nonauthorization
and simple network firewalls.

00:22:23.560 --> 00:22:27.290
So let's jump onto the
scheduling-related features.

00:22:27.290 --> 00:22:29.980
The first one is pod
priority and preemption.

00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:34.720
And the pod priority lets
you define a priority

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:35.770
that the pods get.

00:22:35.770 --> 00:22:37.540
This is a numerical value.

00:22:37.540 --> 00:22:39.580
And depending on
this numerical value,

00:22:39.580 --> 00:22:44.140
the pods are scheduled
earlier than the pods

00:22:44.140 --> 00:22:45.160
with the lower priority.

00:22:45.160 --> 00:22:47.160
So let's say your
cluster is full,

00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:48.730
and there is a scheduling queue.

00:22:48.730 --> 00:22:51.700
The high-priority pod goes in
front of the scheduling queue,

00:22:51.700 --> 00:22:53.600
and it gets scheduled first.

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:57.010
So if you have a bunch of
space issues in your cluster,

00:22:57.010 --> 00:23:00.160
this kind of makes sure that
the high-priority pods actually

00:23:00.160 --> 00:23:01.660
get run first.

00:23:01.660 --> 00:23:03.970
And the pod preemption--
again, these features

00:23:03.970 --> 00:23:07.360
are beta and going through
a beta in Kubernetes 1.11.

00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:10.240
And pod preemption evicts
lower-priority pods

00:23:10.240 --> 00:23:11.120
from a node.

00:23:11.120 --> 00:23:13.060
So let's say your
cluster is full.

00:23:13.060 --> 00:23:15.070
You don't have any more
space, but someone just

00:23:15.070 --> 00:23:16.750
deployed something
super important,

00:23:16.750 --> 00:23:20.184
like the high-priority
web frontend application.

00:23:20.184 --> 00:23:22.600
It just released a new version,
and they need to scale up.

00:23:22.600 --> 00:23:25.900
What happens is that the pod
preemption, which will again

00:23:25.900 --> 00:23:29.710
come in 1.11, it's going to
evict the lower-priority pods

00:23:29.710 --> 00:23:31.120
from the cluster.

00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:32.860
And, again, you know,
this is depending

00:23:32.860 --> 00:23:34.900
on the numerical value.

00:23:34.900 --> 00:23:37.960
In this example here, I
have three priority classes

00:23:37.960 --> 00:23:39.970
that I created, and
one of them is high.

00:23:39.970 --> 00:23:42.410
You know, I just assigned
the value, like 1 million.

00:23:42.410 --> 00:23:44.320
And then there is a normal.

00:23:44.320 --> 00:23:45.444
It has a value of 1,000.

00:23:45.444 --> 00:23:47.110
And then there is a
global default true.

00:23:47.110 --> 00:23:51.180
This means that if I create
a priority class like this,

00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:54.820
this global default true gets
applied to any pod created

00:23:54.820 --> 00:23:58.690
by default. And then
the low has value 10,

00:23:58.690 --> 00:24:00.360
and this is a
non-namespace object.

00:24:00.360 --> 00:24:02.740
So when you apply this to your
cluster, everyone uses it.

00:24:02.740 --> 00:24:06.850
So if you come up with certain
priorities at your company,

00:24:06.850 --> 00:24:10.725
this is something that
you can directly utilize.

00:24:10.725 --> 00:24:13.100
And I'm going to talk a little
bit about resource quotas.

00:24:13.100 --> 00:24:15.890
This is probably the most
important policy object

00:24:15.890 --> 00:24:18.200
if you're trying to limit
usage in your companies

00:24:18.200 --> 00:24:19.590
in a single cluster.

00:24:19.590 --> 00:24:22.130
So resource quotas
are per namespace.

00:24:22.130 --> 00:24:25.550
It lets you limit the
total memory/cpu/storage

00:24:25.550 --> 00:24:28.760
use and the load
balancer use and how many

00:24:28.760 --> 00:24:31.280
pods a namespace can
have, how many config maps

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:36.990
or whatever a namespace
can have for each tenant.

00:24:36.990 --> 00:24:39.540
So here's an example
resource quota.

00:24:39.540 --> 00:24:42.090
In this resource quota, I'm
deploying these to the staging

00:24:42.090 --> 00:24:44.940
namespace, and on the
left, I'm basically

00:24:44.940 --> 00:24:48.810
saying, on this namespace,
you can have only 30 pods.

00:24:48.810 --> 00:24:51.270
And you can have
two total services.

00:24:51.270 --> 00:24:54.180
You cannot have services
with load balancer type,

00:24:54.180 --> 00:24:56.760
because that load balancer
is a public load balancer.

00:24:56.760 --> 00:24:58.980
If I know that this
team is not going

00:24:58.980 --> 00:25:01.660
to need a public load balancer,
I can just disable that.

00:25:01.660 --> 00:25:05.550
And I let this team use
five persistent volumes.

00:25:05.550 --> 00:25:09.420
These are, on GKE, they're
like gcePersistentDisks,

00:25:09.420 --> 00:25:10.920
which is a billable resource.

00:25:10.920 --> 00:25:12.630
In this case, I
can kind of limit

00:25:12.630 --> 00:25:14.110
the spending of this team.

00:25:14.110 --> 00:25:17.280
And on the right, I have the
compute quota of this team.

00:25:17.280 --> 00:25:23.010
I can say, I'm allocating eight
cores of CPU or 10 cores of CPU

00:25:23.010 --> 00:25:26.690
if they're burstable to
this particular tenant.

00:25:26.690 --> 00:25:28.530
And as far as the
RAM is concerned,

00:25:28.530 --> 00:25:31.590
I'm allocating three gigabytes
of RAM to this tenant.

00:25:31.590 --> 00:25:33.940
And, again, I have a
storage field here,

00:25:33.940 --> 00:25:37.740
which basically says that the
total volume of the persistent

00:25:37.740 --> 00:25:40.800
volume claims cannot
go over 120 gigabytes.

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:43.290
So resource quota
is fairly flexible

00:25:43.290 --> 00:25:44.670
and lets you do stuff like that.

00:25:47.440 --> 00:25:51.630
So as of Kubernetes 1.11,
some features going alpha,

00:25:51.630 --> 00:25:53.930
and that feature is
I think Kubernetes

00:25:53.930 --> 00:25:57.900
is allowing you to combine
resource quotas with priority

00:25:57.900 --> 00:25:58.900
classes.

00:25:58.900 --> 00:26:01.080
So if you ever
wondered, oh, what

00:26:01.080 --> 00:26:04.837
if I want to set five CPUs for
my high-priority workloads,

00:26:04.837 --> 00:26:06.420
that's exactly what
you're looking at.

00:26:06.420 --> 00:26:09.060
So on the top left here, I
defined a low-priority class.

00:26:09.060 --> 00:26:11.020
You know, we
defined that before.

00:26:11.020 --> 00:26:14.040
And then here, I'm
basically creating a pod

00:26:14.040 --> 00:26:16.710
with the priority class low.

00:26:16.710 --> 00:26:18.360
That's an unimportant pod.

00:26:18.360 --> 00:26:20.940
And on the right, I'm setting
a resource quota in this

00:26:20.940 --> 00:26:23.710
namespace based on
the priority class,

00:26:23.710 --> 00:26:28.680
so I'm saying that low-priority
class pods can have, in total--

00:26:28.680 --> 00:26:31.350
I can have 100 of these
pods, and they can only

00:26:31.350 --> 00:26:34.090
use a total of 10 CPUs.

00:26:34.090 --> 00:26:37.940
And [INAUDIBLE] you
go buy some memory.

00:26:37.940 --> 00:26:39.740
So one of the most
important features

00:26:39.740 --> 00:26:42.230
you're probably going
to go home and try out

00:26:42.230 --> 00:26:44.180
is the Pod Anti-Affinity.

00:26:44.180 --> 00:26:48.170
So this feature, basically,
if you don't want to co-locate

00:26:48.170 --> 00:26:50.590
tenants on a single node--

00:26:50.590 --> 00:26:52.540
for instance, a lot
of finance companies

00:26:52.540 --> 00:26:53.960
have this requirement--

00:26:53.960 --> 00:26:56.480
this is kind of what
you're looking for.

00:26:56.480 --> 00:27:00.620
Pod Anti-Affinity lets you use
constraints, such as labels,

00:27:00.620 --> 00:27:03.960
to prevent scheduling
on certain nodes.

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:08.120
In this example, I
have a pod that says,

00:27:08.120 --> 00:27:10.520
the label has team
equals billing.

00:27:10.520 --> 00:27:12.770
And on the right,
I have another pod

00:27:12.770 --> 00:27:15.890
that is specifying a
Pod Anti-Affinity role.

00:27:15.890 --> 00:27:18.140
And I'm saying that look
at the Kubernetes hostname.

00:27:18.140 --> 00:27:19.610
This is the node name, right?

00:27:19.610 --> 00:27:21.950
And in this nodename,
don't put me

00:27:21.950 --> 00:27:25.220
on a node that does not
have another pod that

00:27:25.220 --> 00:27:26.850
has the billing label.

00:27:26.850 --> 00:27:29.950
So this lets you, again,
flexibly use these clusters.

00:27:29.950 --> 00:27:33.270
So anytime someone you
know gets scheduled there,

00:27:33.270 --> 00:27:34.910
the next thing you
do is another pod

00:27:34.910 --> 00:27:37.820
that comes in basically
trusts this model.

00:27:37.820 --> 00:27:40.380
And then it will not get
scheduled by Kubernetes

00:27:40.380 --> 00:27:42.140
under any circumstance
to that node.

00:27:45.100 --> 00:27:48.020
Another concept that you
might utilize, especially

00:27:48.020 --> 00:27:50.750
if you have different sorts of
hardware in your data center,

00:27:50.750 --> 00:27:52.289
is dedicated nodes.

00:27:52.289 --> 00:27:54.080
Let's say you have a
machine learning team.

00:27:54.080 --> 00:27:56.380
They ordered a bunch
of GP nodes, right?

00:27:56.380 --> 00:27:58.100
Or they're already
using GP nodes

00:27:58.100 --> 00:27:59.810
on Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:27:59.810 --> 00:28:02.600
And in this case, you
can reserve these nodes

00:28:02.600 --> 00:28:05.420
for the machine learning
team, and then no one else

00:28:05.420 --> 00:28:07.200
will be able to schedule to it.

00:28:07.200 --> 00:28:10.580
And the way this works is we're
using taints and tolerations

00:28:10.580 --> 00:28:14.200
feature on Google
Kubernetes Engine.

00:28:14.200 --> 00:28:19.080
So normally what you do is you
run the command kubectl taint

00:28:19.080 --> 00:28:21.040
node and then
specify the nodename

00:28:21.040 --> 00:28:22.690
to put a taint on the node.

00:28:22.690 --> 00:28:26.200
But in GKE case, you can
directly apply these tenants

00:28:26.200 --> 00:28:27.190
to the node pools.

00:28:27.190 --> 00:28:29.200
So anytime the node
pool scales up,

00:28:29.200 --> 00:28:32.710
or a node gets removed
because of other repair

00:28:32.710 --> 00:28:34.150
or a scale-down
event or something

00:28:34.150 --> 00:28:37.390
like that, all of the nodes
in that node pool will always

00:28:37.390 --> 00:28:40.400
have this team equals
machine-learning NoSchedule

00:28:40.400 --> 00:28:42.130
tenant.

00:28:42.130 --> 00:28:45.850
So GKE offers you this
flexibility to do that.

00:28:45.850 --> 00:28:49.230
And as far as the
tolerations are concerned,

00:28:49.230 --> 00:28:52.830
any pod that wants to
schedule on this node

00:28:52.830 --> 00:28:55.350
needs to have this
particular toleration.

00:28:55.350 --> 00:28:57.360
As you see on the bottom
right, that's saying,

00:28:57.360 --> 00:28:59.280
machine-learning
effect NoSchedule.

00:28:59.280 --> 00:29:02.250
And you know, the team
equals machine-learning

00:29:02.250 --> 00:29:04.050
I'm going to use the
effect NoSchedule.

00:29:04.050 --> 00:29:05.750
So when you use
that, your pod gets

00:29:05.750 --> 00:29:07.530
scheduled onto the GPU node.

00:29:11.890 --> 00:29:14.250
So again, I'm going
to call Yoshi to here,

00:29:14.250 --> 00:29:16.809
and he's going to talk about
Sandboxed pods and gVisor.

00:29:16.809 --> 00:29:18.100
YOSHI TAMURA: Thank you, Ahmet.

00:29:18.100 --> 00:29:19.910
Awesome.

00:29:19.910 --> 00:29:22.000
Before I actually jump
in, how many of you

00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:24.670
actually came to my
gVisor talk, which

00:29:24.670 --> 00:29:26.370
happened like 30 minutes ago?

00:29:26.370 --> 00:29:26.870
So half?

00:29:26.870 --> 00:29:27.389
OK, great.

00:29:27.389 --> 00:29:28.930
So for those, sorry,
it's going to be

00:29:28.930 --> 00:29:30.100
a little bit of repetition.

00:29:30.100 --> 00:29:31.520
But if you have any
questions later,

00:29:31.520 --> 00:29:32.936
we could definitely
talk about it.

00:29:32.936 --> 00:29:37.750
So Sandboxed pods, I'm going to
talk about a lot of primitives

00:29:37.750 --> 00:29:41.710
that we have today in Kubernetes
and Kubernetes Engine.

00:29:41.710 --> 00:29:44.950
Sandboxed pods is
something that we

00:29:44.950 --> 00:29:47.470
are building today and
hopefully to address

00:29:47.470 --> 00:29:52.060
the fundamental problem around
a container isolation boundary.

00:29:52.060 --> 00:29:53.827
There are two
approaches right now,

00:29:53.827 --> 00:29:55.660
which we're going to
talk to you guys about.

00:29:55.660 --> 00:29:57.770
But before that, there are two.

00:29:57.770 --> 00:30:01.560
One is-- Kata Container
is one that will hopefully

00:30:01.560 --> 00:30:04.600
work with the Sandbox pod API.

00:30:04.600 --> 00:30:08.920
And we have gVisor as a
runtime for the Sandbox API.

00:30:08.920 --> 00:30:14.200
The concept of Sandbox is really
about making containers really

00:30:14.200 --> 00:30:17.320
contain to address
the threat that's

00:30:17.320 --> 00:30:22.630
coming from inside, which we'll
talk for now very shortly.

00:30:22.630 --> 00:30:27.940
So gVisor, this is the Google
approach to the Sandbox pods.

00:30:27.940 --> 00:30:31.000
So gVisor is a
kind of new runtime

00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:34.360
for containers to really make it
contained inside this Sandbox.

00:30:37.260 --> 00:30:40.380
Today, when you run
containers, containers

00:30:40.380 --> 00:30:43.410
will directly interact
with the host Linux

00:30:43.410 --> 00:30:47.865
kernel and the system interface
there, like sys calls and slash

00:30:47.865 --> 00:30:48.990
[INAUDIBLE] system.

00:30:48.990 --> 00:30:53.190
You have a very huge
attacking surface there.

00:30:53.190 --> 00:30:58.950
And if a malicious application
inside the container

00:30:58.950 --> 00:31:02.850
exploits the bugs in the
kernel and then escapes

00:31:02.850 --> 00:31:04.980
that container, then basically--

00:31:04.980 --> 00:31:07.380
and then the privileged
discussion happens,

00:31:07.380 --> 00:31:10.460
then basically your system
could be compromised.

00:31:10.460 --> 00:31:13.020
Now, as you can see
from this diagram,

00:31:13.020 --> 00:31:17.210
gVisor will sit in the
middle, and it by itself

00:31:17.210 --> 00:31:19.060
is an independent kernel.

00:31:19.060 --> 00:31:25.470
It implements the Linux
system calls in user space.

00:31:25.470 --> 00:31:27.270
By saying that, what
I mean by the user

00:31:27.270 --> 00:31:32.430
space is that container would
still think that, oh, my system

00:31:32.430 --> 00:31:35.460
calls are handled properly,
but what it's actually

00:31:35.460 --> 00:31:38.680
interacting with is the
system that the user

00:31:38.680 --> 00:31:43.205
in the independent kernel that
is running inside the user

00:31:43.205 --> 00:31:43.750
space.

00:31:43.750 --> 00:31:46.920
So what's going to happen is
that, even if the gVisor--

00:31:46.920 --> 00:31:50.550
well, it's just one other
software-- has a vulnerability,

00:31:50.550 --> 00:31:52.110
and this malicious
software happened

00:31:52.110 --> 00:31:55.410
to be able to take advantage
of that and breaks in.

00:31:55.410 --> 00:31:59.640
Now, what's going to happen is
that basically, it only just

00:31:59.640 --> 00:32:00.860
broke the one boundary.

00:32:00.860 --> 00:32:02.400
You'll still have
another boundary

00:32:02.400 --> 00:32:05.520
between gVisor and kernel.

00:32:05.520 --> 00:32:07.740
Another aspect about
gVisor is that there

00:32:07.740 --> 00:32:11.835
are a lot of tools that
would harden Linux and reduce

00:32:11.835 --> 00:32:16.020
a system surface, such as
seccomp filter and SELinux.

00:32:16.020 --> 00:32:17.760
The challenge there
is that you do

00:32:17.760 --> 00:32:20.880
need to know what you're
doing for your application

00:32:20.880 --> 00:32:22.260
and your users.

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:25.050
And even if you're
able to do so,

00:32:25.050 --> 00:32:27.840
it doesn't mean that
you can use the policy

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:29.400
for every application.

00:32:29.400 --> 00:32:32.250
You have to mandate
that to the application

00:32:32.250 --> 00:32:34.820
to follow that guideline,
or then you can lower--

00:32:34.820 --> 00:32:36.060
or you lower the bar.

00:32:36.060 --> 00:32:38.430
So what's going to
happen, and frequently is

00:32:38.430 --> 00:32:40.680
that if you try to
cover more application,

00:32:40.680 --> 00:32:44.890
more use cases, then you'll step
back, step back a little bit.

00:32:44.890 --> 00:32:48.840
And then you have much more less
described in policies or rules

00:32:48.840 --> 00:32:49.470
there.

00:32:49.470 --> 00:32:51.419
On the other hand,
it's definitely

00:32:51.419 --> 00:32:53.460
going [INAUDIBLE] to
actually come off with that.

00:32:53.460 --> 00:32:55.200
Now, on the other
hand, gVisor, it

00:32:55.200 --> 00:32:57.200
does not have any configuration.

00:32:57.200 --> 00:32:58.470
We'll look into it later.

00:32:58.470 --> 00:32:59.980
But from an
application standpoint,

00:32:59.980 --> 00:33:02.600
you can bring in, you know,
basically any application

00:33:02.600 --> 00:33:06.890
on top of gVisor and run on
top, and you have to modify it.

00:33:06.890 --> 00:33:09.420
But gVisor itself is written
in Go language, which

00:33:09.420 --> 00:33:13.050
is the memory and type safe.

00:33:13.050 --> 00:33:15.090
That's much more
stronger language

00:33:15.090 --> 00:33:19.640
to describe the system
component like gVisor.

00:33:19.640 --> 00:33:21.125
So gVisor on
Kubernetes, how it's

00:33:21.125 --> 00:33:22.750
going to look like
from an architecture

00:33:22.750 --> 00:33:26.960
perspective, gVisor is
going to provide a Sandbox.

00:33:26.960 --> 00:33:29.690
But on top of that,
we have runsc,

00:33:29.690 --> 00:33:33.980
which is OCI runtime that is
actually powered by gVisor.

00:33:33.980 --> 00:33:37.745
So Kubernetes could be talking
into this runsc by OCI,

00:33:37.745 --> 00:33:42.870
or even Docker can talk
to the runsc by OCI.

00:33:42.870 --> 00:33:44.920
As I mentioned,
so underneath it,

00:33:44.920 --> 00:33:49.760
there is-- your container will
be running inside a Sandbox.

00:33:49.760 --> 00:33:53.390
And you'll see, in
this case, the sentry,

00:33:53.390 --> 00:33:56.150
the user LAN kernel
of gVisor, is

00:33:56.150 --> 00:33:59.900
wrapping around that container.

00:33:59.900 --> 00:34:02.090
And then there is
another filter,

00:34:02.090 --> 00:34:04.759
which is seccomp plus
namespace underneath Sentry,

00:34:04.759 --> 00:34:06.050
which we call another boundary.

00:34:06.050 --> 00:34:07.850
So basically, one
isolation and two

00:34:07.850 --> 00:34:10.730
isolation boundaries,
second and first and second.

00:34:10.730 --> 00:34:15.050
To show how it kind of works,
when your container makes

00:34:15.050 --> 00:34:18.889
a system call, then it
will be trapped by the-- so

00:34:18.889 --> 00:34:20.210
we call it a kind of platform.

00:34:20.210 --> 00:34:22.960
I show KVM here, but we
also have [INAUDIBLE]..

00:34:22.960 --> 00:34:25.070
Your sys call will be
captured and forwarded

00:34:25.070 --> 00:34:26.330
to the user LAN kernel.

00:34:26.330 --> 00:34:27.639
So this is a first key.

00:34:27.639 --> 00:34:32.780
Instead of implementing the
system call in the kernel,

00:34:32.780 --> 00:34:35.060
it goes back to the
user LAN and then

00:34:35.060 --> 00:34:37.230
it's implemented as
much as possible.

00:34:37.230 --> 00:34:39.500
There are cases to implement--

00:34:39.500 --> 00:34:42.199
Sentry has to call
the system call

00:34:42.199 --> 00:34:44.510
to implement the
request of sys call,

00:34:44.510 --> 00:34:48.350
but we significantly
filter the actual system

00:34:48.350 --> 00:34:52.250
call that Sentry is going
to make to the Linux kernel.

00:34:52.250 --> 00:34:54.409
And that's exactly where
seccomp comes into play,

00:34:54.409 --> 00:34:57.120
and it's surrounded
by namespace.

00:34:57.120 --> 00:34:59.930
It's around like
10 to 20 sys call

00:34:59.930 --> 00:35:04.134
compared to the full 320-ish
sys call stats exposed

00:35:04.134 --> 00:35:05.300
in the regular Linux kernel.

00:35:05.300 --> 00:35:08.090
So it's much more restricted.

00:35:08.090 --> 00:35:10.430
File I/O and Network
is also another service

00:35:10.430 --> 00:35:12.260
we have to take care of.

00:35:12.260 --> 00:35:15.680
By default, your container
will not have direct access

00:35:15.680 --> 00:35:17.390
to the host file system.

00:35:17.390 --> 00:35:20.930
Everything will be handled
by so-called Gofer, which

00:35:20.930 --> 00:35:22.610
will handle the
file and networking,

00:35:22.610 --> 00:35:29.390
and we have 9P protocol to
connect Gofer and Sentry there.

00:35:33.570 --> 00:35:36.570
So this is, like I mentioned,
the Sandbox pod in Kubernetes.

00:35:36.570 --> 00:35:38.430
This is exactly
what we're working.

00:35:38.430 --> 00:35:41.790
We're leading effort
in the community.

00:35:41.790 --> 00:35:44.850
It is a work in progress, so
it's not something available

00:35:44.850 --> 00:35:45.660
yet.

00:35:45.660 --> 00:35:49.050
But I think we have
a very final stage

00:35:49.050 --> 00:35:51.450
of the spec at this moment.

00:35:51.450 --> 00:35:54.300
Actually, this is really my
third time revising this stack,

00:35:54.300 --> 00:35:56.730
so what I'm showing
now is the latest one.

00:35:56.730 --> 00:35:58.080
There's no guarantee that
it's not going to change.

00:35:58.080 --> 00:36:00.330
I'm pretty sure that things
will change along the way.

00:36:00.330 --> 00:36:02.820
So just treat it as a
grain of salt. And also,

00:36:02.820 --> 00:36:06.050
unlike Ahmet's awesome
YAML file [INAUDIBLE],,

00:36:06.050 --> 00:36:09.735
I'm just going to take advantage
of that to say very simple,

00:36:09.735 --> 00:36:12.240
short YAML file for my talk.

00:36:12.240 --> 00:36:14.970
So a runtime class
is the new API

00:36:14.970 --> 00:36:18.270
to specify runtime so that
we can implement this concept

00:36:18.270 --> 00:36:20.880
called Sandbox pods.

00:36:20.880 --> 00:36:23.940
The left side of this YAML
file is the actual way

00:36:23.940 --> 00:36:27.660
to specify the runtime class,
and you can see the meta name.

00:36:27.660 --> 00:36:30.300
So this runtime class
will be named gVisor,

00:36:30.300 --> 00:36:33.720
and inside there, there's a
runtime handler called gVisor.

00:36:33.720 --> 00:36:35.400
Inside this runtime
handler, you can

00:36:35.400 --> 00:36:39.420
specify the actual executable
file and all the parameters

00:36:39.420 --> 00:36:40.720
in there.

00:36:40.720 --> 00:36:43.370
But we believe that this
left side, the specifying

00:36:43.370 --> 00:36:47.760
a runtime class part, will be
defined by the Cluster Admin.

00:36:47.760 --> 00:36:51.120
Or in case of Cloud, like
us, Kubernetes Engine,

00:36:51.120 --> 00:36:55.380
will be us who will be defining
the runtime class for you.

00:36:55.380 --> 00:36:59.430
And from a user perspective,
all you have to do

00:36:59.430 --> 00:37:02.730
is to specify
which runtime class

00:37:02.730 --> 00:37:04.436
you want to use for your pod.

00:37:04.436 --> 00:37:06.060
So on the right side,
you can see this.

00:37:06.060 --> 00:37:08.190
All you have to do is just
write your regular pod,

00:37:08.190 --> 00:37:12.300
and then say, runtime class name
gVisor, which is provided by--

00:37:12.300 --> 00:37:14.550
which is defined by
the left-hand side.

00:37:14.550 --> 00:37:15.931
So it's very straightforward.

00:37:18.700 --> 00:37:23.160
All righty, so we talked
about multi-tenancy,

00:37:23.160 --> 00:37:25.420
and Ahmet talked
about and discussed

00:37:25.420 --> 00:37:29.280
and hopefully introduced you
to what are the things needed

00:37:29.280 --> 00:37:31.600
to use multi-tenancy.

00:37:31.600 --> 00:37:34.380
But there are some areas
that we're still working on,

00:37:34.380 --> 00:37:36.310
and where the community
is still working on.

00:37:36.310 --> 00:37:40.070
So I wanted to quickly
go through that.

00:37:40.070 --> 00:37:41.450
Policy management.

00:37:41.450 --> 00:37:45.110
I think this is really,
perhaps only myself,

00:37:45.110 --> 00:37:49.280
but when Ahmet was talking
about RBAC and policy and all

00:37:49.280 --> 00:37:54.230
the admin files, you have
to know what you need to do.

00:37:54.230 --> 00:37:58.700
And doing just one is
already complicated enough.

00:37:58.700 --> 00:38:01.790
But if you're responsible for
providing such an environment

00:38:01.790 --> 00:38:05.570
for a larger organization,
with many, many projects,

00:38:05.570 --> 00:38:08.120
yes, it's not going to
be necessarily scalable.

00:38:08.120 --> 00:38:12.870
It is a challenge to make sure
that IAM policy is up to date,

00:38:12.870 --> 00:38:13.370
right?

00:38:13.370 --> 00:38:15.020
Once you define it, great.

00:38:15.020 --> 00:38:17.060
But how do you know
that is actually

00:38:17.060 --> 00:38:19.400
still consistent
in the long run,

00:38:19.400 --> 00:38:21.620
and/or like a different
projects routine?

00:38:21.620 --> 00:38:25.370
So that will be the
challenge, and I'm

00:38:25.370 --> 00:38:29.180
very excited to introduce
this Kubernetes Engine Policy

00:38:29.180 --> 00:38:30.240
Management.

00:38:30.240 --> 00:38:32.330
This is very new, alpha.

00:38:32.330 --> 00:38:35.910
This is the product that my
colleagues are working on.

00:38:35.910 --> 00:38:39.120
And unfortunately, we have
this kind of colliding,

00:38:39.120 --> 00:38:42.920
so the talk is now
happening in Moscone South,

00:38:42.920 --> 00:38:46.070
so I will strongly
recommend to watch the video

00:38:46.070 --> 00:38:47.460
once it gets uploaded.

00:38:47.460 --> 00:38:49.793
But I was just going to tell
you the nutshell, because I

00:38:49.793 --> 00:38:53.630
had a chance to understand the
vision from the TL and the PMs

00:38:53.630 --> 00:38:54.800
yesterday.

00:38:54.800 --> 00:38:57.590
So like I mentioned, the
policy management and scaling,

00:38:57.590 --> 00:39:00.120
that is very, very challenging.

00:39:00.120 --> 00:39:03.740
So the ideal vision here
is that all you have to do

00:39:03.740 --> 00:39:08.030
is come to the Kubernetes
Cloud [INAUDIBLE]..

00:39:08.030 --> 00:39:13.520
And when you specify the project
from your organization folder,

00:39:13.520 --> 00:39:16.610
if you opt in, once
you create a project,

00:39:16.610 --> 00:39:20.900
it will automatically create a
namespace inside your cluster

00:39:20.900 --> 00:39:21.400
for you.

00:39:21.400 --> 00:39:24.230
So like team building
or team development.

00:39:24.230 --> 00:39:27.590
Once you do that, it will
always create a namespace

00:39:27.590 --> 00:39:29.840
with that particular
labeling so that you

00:39:29.840 --> 00:39:31.340
don't have to worry about it.

00:39:31.340 --> 00:39:33.740
So the larger
vision is that, yes,

00:39:33.740 --> 00:39:37.410
you can specify a lot of
those policies here and there,

00:39:37.410 --> 00:39:40.699
but isn't it much more
effective and ideal

00:39:40.699 --> 00:39:42.740
developer and user experience
if we could provide

00:39:42.740 --> 00:39:44.790
a cohesive kind of view there?

00:39:44.790 --> 00:39:48.560
And that's what it's all about
with this Kubernetes Engine

00:39:48.560 --> 00:39:50.270
Policy Management.

00:39:50.270 --> 00:39:51.900
So two things.

00:39:51.900 --> 00:39:56.090
So the talk I/O
200 happening now.

00:39:56.090 --> 00:40:01.012
I strongly recommend watching
this once it gets uploaded.

00:40:01.012 --> 00:40:01.970
Also, this is in alpha.

00:40:01.970 --> 00:40:05.690
They absolutely want somebody
to sign up and also feedback,

00:40:05.690 --> 00:40:10.160
so please come to this, click,
go through this sign-up form

00:40:10.160 --> 00:40:15.750
if you're interested in
scaling your policy management.

00:40:15.750 --> 00:40:21.250
Also, Kubernetes multi-tenancy
has some limitation.

00:40:21.250 --> 00:40:26.800
One is that for now, the API
calls are not rate limited.

00:40:26.800 --> 00:40:30.990
So you can see that this could
be perhaps one of the attacking

00:40:30.990 --> 00:40:36.240
surface, and we need to
address that in the long run.

00:40:36.240 --> 00:40:39.480
And also, networking is also
not kind of scheduled resources

00:40:39.480 --> 00:40:43.287
yet, so we do need
to figure out what

00:40:43.287 --> 00:40:45.870
will be the right way to kind
of slice our networking resource

00:40:45.870 --> 00:40:49.260
and attach it to the namespace.

00:40:49.260 --> 00:40:50.940
And also, tenants
can still discover

00:40:50.940 --> 00:40:53.640
each other via the
Kubernetes DNS,

00:40:53.640 --> 00:40:56.050
so these are at a kind
of a high-level control

00:40:56.050 --> 00:41:00.510
plane, robustness and
isolation that we will probably

00:41:00.510 --> 00:41:03.900
need if we want to implement
the full Kubernetes

00:41:03.900 --> 00:41:08.790
multi-tenancy, the model
that Ahmet introduced earlier

00:41:08.790 --> 00:41:09.550
in his slides.

00:41:12.310 --> 00:41:18.600
OK, so key takeaway for the
multi-tenancy in general.

00:41:18.600 --> 00:41:20.914
First, define it.

00:41:20.914 --> 00:41:22.330
It's a very kind
of rule of thumb,

00:41:22.330 --> 00:41:24.010
but I think it's very important.

00:41:24.010 --> 00:41:26.110
Determine your use case.

00:41:26.110 --> 00:41:30.730
Is your user coming from
outside your organization

00:41:30.730 --> 00:41:32.350
or inside your organization?

00:41:32.350 --> 00:41:33.890
That's one way to look at it.

00:41:33.890 --> 00:41:36.890
But perhaps much more
[INAUDIBLE],, how trusted

00:41:36.890 --> 00:41:40.860
are your tenant and
users and workflows?

00:41:40.860 --> 00:41:43.100
This is actually
perhaps more important.

00:41:43.100 --> 00:41:46.060
If you trust everyone
correctly, and if you

00:41:46.060 --> 00:41:48.490
trust whatever software
that they're going to bring,

00:41:48.490 --> 00:41:52.660
I think you have a
better position not

00:41:52.660 --> 00:41:55.210
to use all the isolation
mechanism or policy

00:41:55.210 --> 00:41:56.140
intensively.

00:41:56.140 --> 00:41:59.050
But if not, you
do need something.

00:41:59.050 --> 00:42:04.180
And related to that, what sort
of a degree of isolation do you

00:42:04.180 --> 00:42:04.820
want to have?

00:42:04.820 --> 00:42:08.342
Is it per the cluster level,
or the taint and toleration

00:42:08.342 --> 00:42:09.550
that I'm going to talk about?

00:42:09.550 --> 00:42:10.930
You know, it's node level.

00:42:10.930 --> 00:42:14.450
Or we actually want to
even split inside a node.

00:42:14.450 --> 00:42:16.450
And if you're looking for
that, yes, Sandbox pod

00:42:16.450 --> 00:42:20.420
will be definitely something
you want to think about.

00:42:20.420 --> 00:42:26.510
And the namespace-specific
multi-tenancy, we do

00:42:26.510 --> 00:42:29.210
understand, as of now,
that namespace is still

00:42:29.210 --> 00:42:31.220
perhaps a newer concept.

00:42:31.220 --> 00:42:34.340
For those who are already
kind of using namespace,

00:42:34.340 --> 00:42:38.060
I think it's totally fair
to think that namespace

00:42:38.060 --> 00:42:39.740
is the kind of
starting point to think

00:42:39.740 --> 00:42:42.350
about the multi-tenancy in
Kubernetes and Kubernetes

00:42:42.350 --> 00:42:43.680
Engine.

00:42:43.680 --> 00:42:47.030
But I also understand that there
are people who are actually

00:42:47.030 --> 00:42:51.020
running multi-tenancy
services, so Team A, Team B

00:42:51.020 --> 00:42:54.440
running on the same cluster,
but not using namespace.

00:42:54.440 --> 00:42:58.490
That's totally fine, but I
definitely do recommend using--

00:42:58.490 --> 00:43:01.610
considering namespace
moving forward.

00:43:01.610 --> 00:43:05.390
And here, we wanted to utilize
a policy object and scheduling

00:43:05.390 --> 00:43:07.430
and access control.

00:43:07.430 --> 00:43:10.040
And also, think
about the persona

00:43:10.040 --> 00:43:16.040
and how you want it to map
into the RBAC roles in there.

00:43:16.040 --> 00:43:21.080
And if you have more
complicated-- now, of course,

00:43:21.080 --> 00:43:23.450
the problem is very complex
enough for a single cluster.

00:43:23.450 --> 00:43:27.410
But if you actually do want to
have more robust and great user

00:43:27.410 --> 00:43:31.100
experience from single
cluster to multiple cluster,

00:43:31.100 --> 00:43:34.170
the GKE, Google Kubernetes
Engine Policy Management

00:43:34.170 --> 00:43:38.850
that has been announced now
will be a great choice for you.

00:43:38.850 --> 00:43:41.356
So I strongly recommend
looking into this.

00:43:41.356 --> 00:43:45.420
OK, so that's it, I think.

00:43:45.420 --> 00:43:49.880
So, Ahmet, you wanted to talk
about the call for actions.

00:43:49.880 --> 00:43:51.570
AHMET ALP BALKAN:
Yeah, in this case,

00:43:51.570 --> 00:43:53.420
we want you to
bring your opinions

00:43:53.420 --> 00:43:55.290
to the multi-tenancy space.

00:43:55.290 --> 00:43:58.820
So for that, I'll keep this
slide up for a bit so you can

00:43:58.820 --> 00:44:04.190
take a picture of that and go
to gke.page.link/multi-tenancy.

00:44:04.190 --> 00:44:07.060
And fill out this form to
register your interest.

00:44:07.060 --> 00:44:08.060
What do you want to see?

00:44:08.060 --> 00:44:09.040
What is your use case?

00:44:09.040 --> 00:44:11.066
We really want to
hear about this.

00:44:11.066 --> 00:44:12.440
And in the open
source community,

00:44:12.440 --> 00:44:13.880
the work is still happening.

00:44:13.880 --> 00:44:16.790
When we looked at the
Kubernetes space a year ago,

00:44:16.790 --> 00:44:19.252
there were only a few
of the policy objects

00:44:19.252 --> 00:44:20.960
that we were talking
about, and now we're

00:44:20.960 --> 00:44:24.140
at a state where most of
the policy objects are in--

00:44:24.140 --> 00:44:26.630
some of them are already GA,
such as the resource quota.

00:44:26.630 --> 00:44:28.880
And some of them are going
beta, and some of them

00:44:28.880 --> 00:44:31.130
are coming up very
recently as alpha.

00:44:31.130 --> 00:44:32.576
So we want also
you to, if you're

00:44:32.576 --> 00:44:33.950
interested in this
space, we want

00:44:33.950 --> 00:44:36.245
you to participate in the
open source community.

00:44:36.245 --> 00:44:39.190
So most prominently, the
relevant working group

00:44:39.190 --> 00:44:41.780
in Kubernetes space is going
to be the multi-tenancy working

00:44:41.780 --> 00:44:43.070
group.

00:44:43.070 --> 00:44:45.590
And in this case,
this work group

00:44:45.590 --> 00:44:48.170
is led by engineers from
Google and Microsoft.

00:44:48.170 --> 00:44:52.170
And you know, you can find links
here to join this work group.

00:44:52.170 --> 00:44:54.590
Similarly, the policies
are very key to Kubernetes

00:44:54.590 --> 00:44:57.890
multi-tenancy, and the
Kubernetes policy working group

00:44:57.890 --> 00:44:59.840
is looking at these
sort of policies.

00:44:59.840 --> 00:45:03.620
Basically, how we can use the
namespace logical boundary

00:45:03.620 --> 00:45:05.630
and take it to a
place where people

00:45:05.630 --> 00:45:08.300
can use it to
achieve multi-tenancy

00:45:08.300 --> 00:45:09.110
in their clusters.

00:45:09.110 --> 00:45:10.910
Again, the Kubernetes
policy working group

00:45:10.910 --> 00:45:12.920
is a pretty fun
place to hang out.

00:45:12.920 --> 00:45:15.110
I would totally recommend
reading their design docs.

00:45:15.110 --> 00:45:16.693
And there are two
other working groups

00:45:16.693 --> 00:45:18.480
that I have not listed here.

00:45:18.480 --> 00:45:21.175
One of them is SIG Auth,
which looks at authorization

00:45:21.175 --> 00:45:22.720
and authentication.

00:45:22.720 --> 00:45:26.420
If you're interested in RBAC and
IAM and how these things work,

00:45:26.420 --> 00:45:28.250
and if you want to
bring, I don't know,

00:45:28.250 --> 00:45:30.740
your own YDC provider
and stuff like

00:45:30.740 --> 00:45:33.770
that, if you want to provide
opinions in that area,

00:45:33.770 --> 00:45:35.990
SIG Auth is where
you should look at.

00:45:35.990 --> 00:45:38.900
Again, another one
is SIG Scheduling.

00:45:38.900 --> 00:45:41.750
As the name indicates,
they bring a lot of stuff

00:45:41.750 --> 00:45:44.940
from the policy work
group, and already

00:45:44.940 --> 00:45:47.480
stuff that exists in
Kubernetes, such as I've

00:45:47.480 --> 00:45:50.401
talked about how the pod
preemption and pod priorities

00:45:50.401 --> 00:45:50.900
work.

00:45:50.900 --> 00:45:54.666
This is SIG scheduling, so you
should definitely check out

00:45:54.666 --> 00:45:55.790
the SIG Scheduling as well.

00:45:55.790 --> 00:45:58.970
If you're generally interested
in distributed systems

00:45:58.970 --> 00:46:01.734
scheduling space, SIG
Scheduling, again,

00:46:01.734 --> 00:46:02.900
is a nice place to hang out.

00:46:02.900 --> 00:46:06.290
And there are a lot of
interesting design docs there.

00:46:06.290 --> 00:46:07.700
So this concludes our talk.

00:46:07.700 --> 00:46:09.690
Thanks again for coming up.

00:46:09.690 --> 00:46:12.170
Again, this link is still
going to be there, so feel

00:46:12.170 --> 00:46:13.970
free to take pictures of that.

00:46:13.970 --> 00:46:17.220
If you have questions, I think
we have only a few minutes.

00:46:17.220 --> 00:46:19.010
But we can get
questions at the mic,

00:46:19.010 --> 00:46:21.320
but we'll make ourselves
available here after the talk

00:46:21.320 --> 00:46:21.820
as well.

00:46:21.820 --> 00:46:23.900
So please don't hesitate
to ask your questions.

00:46:23.900 --> 00:46:25.220
Again, thank you very much.

00:46:25.220 --> 00:46:26.220
YOSHI TAMURA: Thank you.

00:46:26.220 --> 00:46:27.170
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:30.170 --> 00:46:33.520
